'Réponds en français. Are the MEANPOT values normally distributed in the dataset?\n\nConstraints : Determine the normality of the values in the MEANPOT column using the Shapiro-Wilk test for normality. Consider the values to be normally distributed if the p-value is greater than 0.05. Report your findings as "Normal" if the p-value is greater than 0.05 and "Not Normal" otherwise. Report the p-value as well.\n\nAnswer using EXACTLY this format, adding nothing to it:\n@normality_test_result[A string that should be either "Normal" or "Not Normal"] @p_value[The Shapiro-Wilk test p-value, rounded to three decimal places]', (15872, 401)
'Réponds en français. What is the mean batting average of the players in the dataset?\n\nConstraints : Consider only the players who have a recorded batting average. Do not include the one player with a missing batting average into your calculation. Report your answer rounded off to three decimal places.\n\nAnswer using EXACTLY this format, adding nothing to it:\n@mean_batting_average[mean_batting_average] where "mean_batting_average" is a float between 0 and 1, rounded to three decimal places.', (17408, 399)
'Réponds en français. Create a new feature called "batting_average_minus_on_base_percentage" which represents the difference between a player\'s batting average and their on-base percentage. Calculate the mean and standard deviation of this new feature.\n\nConstraints : To calculate the new feature, subtract each player\'s on-base percentage from their batting average. Ignore the missing values and areas with null values for batting average or on-base percentage. Calculate both the mean and standard deviation using these new feature values.\n\nAnswer using EXACTLY this format, adding nothing to it:\n@mean[mean_value] @std_dev[std_dev_value] where "mean_value" is the mean of the new feature, and "std_dev_value" is the standard deviation of the new feature. Both should be rounded to two decimal places.', (17920, 645)
